  "result": "The lesson is written to `lessons/0001-context-and-memory.html`. It is for objective O3 and set at 30 minutes. I haven't opened it in a browser or run any checks on it. I didn't count its words either, but by my section plan it should land near 30 minutes.\n\nIt covers four concepts, each opening with a traced example in the makerspace setting:\n1. **The context window.** The model sees only the tokens in the current call.\n2. **What is kept between steps.** Your program re-sends the whole history on every call, so tokens and cost grow. The worked trace goes 1,130 → 1,590 → 3,640 input tokens.\n3. **What is kept between sessions: notes.** A small Python notes file carries a student's training status from Monday to Thursday.\n4. **Retrieval.** A toy search over manual chunks puts only the matching page into the context.\n\nEach concept has a \"Why this works\" box and a multiple-choice check with immediate feedback. The lesson also has a warm-up recall question on the agent loop and two common-mix-up boxes. It ends with a worked example and practice on where information should live (context, notes or a searchable manual). I also updated `NOTES.md`. I added a `RESOURCES.md`; its sources are Anthropic's context-engineering post, the context-windows docs and the contextual-retrieval post, all from this session's searches.\n\n- **Not covered:** compaction gets only a short side note, and how retrieval scores chunks (embeddings, BM25) is left for a later lesson.\n- **Community:** I found no outside community with a verified link, so \"test it with others\" points to your own design team.\n\nAsk me for a quiz on this lesson when you're ready, or ask me anything about it. Quiz 0001 from the agent-loop session is also still ungraded, so I can grade it whenever you like.",
